  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nLow pressure will move into the James Bay region this afternoon and \nbegin to weaken through the evening. The associated cold front will \nmove northwest to southeast across the region becoming less defined \nas it moves across Lake Erie. The front itself will be fairly dry \noutside of far Northeastern Ohio and Northwestern Pennsylvania. The \nmajority of the precipitation that occurs will be lake effect driven \nas the lake is still relatively warm compared to the air moving in \nbehind the cold front. 850mb temperatures across that portion \nof the area will drop down to near 0C during the day on Monday. \nThough, most of the moisture will exit to the east by midday \nMonday as the 925mb ridge begins to build in and dwindle the \nlake effect into the evening. Generally, not expecting much \naccumulation across Northwestern Pennsylvania, around a couple \nof tenths of an inch at most with all of it staying as rain with\nthe warmer lower levels. A more widespread side effect to this \nsystem, will be the gusty winds into the evening today. Winds \nwill generally be out of the west- southwest ahead of the cold \nfront at 10-20 MPH with gusts around 25- 30 MPH and occasionally\nup to 30-35 MPH. Winds will subside this evening. \n\nHigh pressure will build in across the Ohio Valley during the day \nMonday and persist into Tuesday before building off the east coast. \nDry weather is expected during that time with relatively seasonable \ntemperatures, if not a few degrees warmer than average. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nAs high pressure departs to the east on Wednesday, low pressure will \nbegin to deepen to the west across the Mid-Mississippi Valley. \nThere's still some discrepancies in the models on the exact track \nand magnitude of the low as it enters the southern Great Lakes \nregion. Though confidence is high that there will be widespread \nprecipitation beginning late Wednesday evening through Thursday \nnight. The low pressure will depart off to the northeast Friday into \nSaturday and persistent north to northwesterly flow will set up \nacross the region. There will be a much cooler air mass moving in \nbehind the low as well dropping highs down into the mid to low 40s \nand overnight lows into the upper 20s to low 30s. With that, \nconfidence is increasing in prolonged lake effect precipitation \nexpected into the weekend. And with the colder air mass moving in, \nthere is potential for a rain/snow mix this weekend across Northeast \nOhio and Northwestern Pennsylvania. \n\nIt is still unknown how long the lake effect will persist as \nmodels disagree on how quickly the low exits and the oncoming \nridge builds into the region. The AI models of the ECMWF and GFS\nare trending to hold onto the lake effect for longer, lasting \ninto early next weeks. Whereas the non-AI version of those \nmodels move the precipitation out quicker. Depending on the \nsolution will determine if there will be any rain/snow mix or if\nthere will be mainly rain. At this point, confidence is low to \nsay either way.",
